  "summary": "Late-January 2026 preprints apply reinforcement learning and objective guidance to pretrained generative models for RNA and peptide design.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these four preprints is steering pretrained generative models for biomolecular sequences toward explicit objectives rather than sampling unconditionally. SOLD applies reinforcement learning over a latent diffusion model with RNA-FM embeddings, optimizing secondary structure, minimum free energy, and LDDT for RNA inverse folding. ProDCARL aligns an EvoDiff peptide generator with rewards from antimicrobial activity and toxicity predictors. MadSBM uses a rate-based discrete Schrödinger bridge with protein language model priors and functional guidance for peptide sequence design. UCCS instead applies utility-controlled contrastive steering to reduce repetition in ESM-3 and ProtGPT2. What is new is objective-driven conditioning applied across RNA and antimicrobial peptide design, with mechanisms that differ between items.",
